"The postoperative pathology is reassuring, with the surgical pathology report noting, \"surgical margins were negative; the seminal vesicles were not invaded; lymphovascular invasion was absent; no lymph nodes were removed.\" The preoperative findings included a Gleason 3+4 (ISUP grade group 2) and pathological stage pT2c. This favorable surgical outcome is contrasted by the preoperative findings, which included a Gleason 3+3 diagnosis from the biopsies. The absence of documented nodal sampling means that the pNx status does not establish node-negative disease, which widens the uncertainty regarding regional disease control. The absence of a recorded postoperative PSA means biochemical recurrence cannot be assessed. Information regarding postoperative PSA and its subsequent trajectory is crucial for revising the prognosis. The current findings suggest a favorable surgical resection, but the lack of nodal status information limits the completeness of the staging. The fixed estimated horizon is 71.94 months. This represents expected follow-up without an event, not a known recurrence date. The sensitivity range is 43.16–100.71 months; its coverage has not been validated."
